[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> 1. The string matching occurs at the packet level, not at the HTTP header level
[?2004l[?2004h> 2. Many legitimate HTTP clients (curl, wget, custom applications, API clients, monitoring tools) do not include "Mozilla" in their User-Agent strings
[?2004l[?2004h> 3. The DROP rules (lines 9-10) catch all HTTP/HTTPS traffic that doesn't match the "Mozilla" string, causing connections to hang
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> WHY SSH WORKS BUT HTTP DOESN'T:
[?2004l[?2004h> - SSH (port 22) has an unconditional ACCEPT rule with no string matching requirements (line 6)
[?2004l[?2004h> - HTTP/HTTPS require the "Mozilla" string to be present in the packet payload
[?2004l[?2004h> - When curl connects to ports 80/443, it sends a User-Agent like "curl/7.x.x" which doesn't contain "Mozilla"
[?2004l[?2004h> - The traffic doesn't match the ACCEPT rule (lines 7-8) and falls through to the DROP rules (lines 9-10)
[?2004l[?2004h> - The connection hangs because packets are silently dropped rather than rejected
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> RECOMMENDATION:
[?2004l[?2004h> This filtering approach is fundamentally flawed for several reasons:
[?2004l[?2004h> - String matching at the packet level is unreliable for HTTP headers (headers may span multiple packets)
[?2004l[?2004h> - It breaks legitimate non-browser HTTP clients
[?2004l[?2004h> - It's easily bypassed by adding "Mozilla" to any User-Agent string
[?2004l[?2004h> - It provides no real security benefit
[?2004l[?2004h> 
